.th PARA I July/76
.sh NAME
para \*- improved C paragrapher and syntax checker
.sh SYNOPSIS
.bd para
file.c
.sh DESCRIPTION
A paragraphed listing of the named "C" program is produced on the
standard output.
Syntax errors are reported on the standard diagnostic output.
Thus, to syntax check a program, it suffices to issue the command:

.ti 10
para file.c >/dev/null

The syntax enforced is somewhat stricter than that enforced
by the C compiler.
Also, the preprocessor enforces the 8 character identifier
rule strictly.
(The standard "cc" preprocessor will only do so occasionally.)

Programs with syntax errors will still be paragraphed, but they will
look ugly near the point of the error.

The input and output should not be the same, or you will come to grief.
.sh FILES
.sh "SEE ALSO"
cc(I), csyn(VI), C Reference Manual
.sh DIAGNOSTICS
Of course.
Messages which say "Fatal error" mean you
have hit upon some internal limitation.
.sh AUTHOR
Mike Tilson
.sh BUGS
Error recovery, while generally better than that of the C compiler, is still not
as good as it might be.

If you have very long lines, they can get broken in places which will
cause the resulting program to not compile.
This is a bug in the C compiler, i.e.:
.ti 10
function
.ti 10
(x);
.br
will not compile because the newline is not treated strictly as a blank.

If any input token is longer than 100 or so characters, you can dump core.

In certain cases, macro processing can prevent newlines from being output
where they should.
for example,

.nf
#define cycle for(;;)
#define x y
main() {
	cycle x;
}

instead of:

main() {
	cycle
		x;
}

.fi
which is correct.
The problem occurs when there are two
macros in a row, one of which causes a new line.
This is fairly hard to fix, because you do not
want new lines from inside macros.
